\section*{1. Project Objective}

This project aims to partially reproduce the findings
of Goyal et al.~\cite{goyal2023}, who investigated
mimicry attacks against provenance graph host intrusion
detection systems (Prov-HIDS). We focus on Pagoda,
one of the detection systems evaluated in the original
study.

Our objective is to determine whether mimicry-modified
attack graphs are less likely to be detected than
unmodified attack graphs. This is relevant to network
and systems security because intrusion detection
mechanisms must remain effective against adversaries
who deliberately disguise malicious behavior as
legitimate system activity.

\section*{2. Methodology}

We used the researchers' publicly released implementation
and provenance graph datasets~\cite{repository}.
The experimental environment consisted of Windows 11,
Python 3.11.9, PyTorch running on CPU, and 16 GB RAM.
We installed the required Python dependencies and
prepared CSV graph files using custom extraction scripts.

We first conducted a small pilot to verify that the
Pagoda pipeline could execute successfully. We then
performed two experiments using different amounts of
benign training data: five graphs and the full set of
71 available benign training graphs.

For both configurations, we evaluated five original
attack graphs and five mimicry-evasion graphs.
We also implemented a separate evaluation script
to test five previously unseen benign graphs using
the saved Pagoda scoring artifacts and thresholds.

During implementation, we addressed dependency
configuration, dataset extraction, intermediate
artifact management, and interpretation of Pagoda's
output counters. We preserved the original detection
implementation and added scripts for dataset preparation
and held-out evaluation.

\section*{3. Results}

Table~\ref{tab:results} summarizes the experimental
outcomes.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Pagoda reproduction results}
\label{tab:results}
\begin{tabular}{p{2.95in}cc}
\toprule
\textbf{Evaluation metric}
& \textbf{5 train}
& \textbf{71 train}\\
\midrule
Original attacks detected
& 5/5 & 5/5\\

Mimicry-evasion graphs detected
& 5/5 & 0/5\\

Mimicry-evasion graphs escaping detection
& 0/5 & 5/5\\

Training benign graphs flagged
& 0/5 & 0/71\\

Held-out benign graphs incorrectly flagged
& 5/5 & 5/5\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

With five benign training graphs, Pagoda detected
both the original attacks and the mimicry-modified
graphs. However, after increasing the benign training
set to 71 graphs, Pagoda continued detecting all five
original attacks while failing to detect all five
mimicry-evasion graphs.

This qualitatively supports the original paper's
central finding that mimicry attacks can evade
provenance-based intrusion detection~\cite{goyal2023}.
Our experiment demonstrated this behavior for Pagoda
on a smaller subset, rather than reproducing the
full study's quantitative evaluation.

Importantly, all five held-out benign graphs were
incorrectly flagged as attacks under our evaluation
procedure. The training-benign counts in
Table~\ref{tab:results} are in-sample observations
and should not be interpreted as independent
false-positive rates.

\section*{4. Discussion}

Our results suggest that Pagoda's response to
mimicry-modified attacks can vary with the training
configuration. Increasing the benign training set
from five to 71 graphs coincided with a change
in mimicry detection from 5/5 to 0/5. However,
this comparison alone does not establish causation.

The most significant limitation was the 100\%
false-positive rate observed on five held-out benign
graphs. All five were flagged by the graph-level
threshold. This may reflect differences between
our evaluation procedure and the original experimental
protocol, including threshold selection or scoring.

Another limitation is that the original Pagoda
implementation uses attack scores during threshold
calibration. Consequently, detection results for
those same attack graphs are not fully independent.
Our evaluation covered only five original attacks,
five evasion graphs, and five held-out benign graphs,
limiting generalizability.

Overall, we successfully executed Pagoda and partially
reproduced the paper's central mimicry-evasion behavior.
However, we could not establish reliable overall
detection performance. The project highlighted the
importance of independent evaluation data, threshold
calibration, and careful interpretation of security
detection metrics.

